WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.680
I was born and grew up in Denmark and these are countries where the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.336
spends taxes and spends approximately twice the&nbsp;level of the United States.

00:00:10.496 --> 00:00:14.754
And while I am very sympathetic to many of your spending proposals,

00:00:14.754 --> 00:00:19.085
especially on the things you mentioned on early childhood and single-payer and the like,

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:24.478
I also&nbsp;know that these are countries that heavily tax everybody.

00:00:24.478 --> 00:00:31.280
Not just the rich people, middle classes&nbsp;
they have consumption taxes on everything of 20%

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.800
So while I'm very sympathetic to what you say, my sense is still that you would like to spend as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.192
a Scandinavian but not taxed as one. Is that correct&nbsp;?

00:00:40.192 --> 00:00:44.891
We have the answers and you raise some very good points...

00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:51.898
Comparing the US with Europe and countries&nbsp;in Europe would be naive, over-simplistic and offend a bunch of people.

00:00:51.898 --> 00:00:56.771
Land masses are complex but&nbsp;
I'm going to go ahead and compare them anyways,

00:00:56.771 --> 00:01:01.177
because some of the comparisons between Europe&nbsp;and the US remain way too superficial.

00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:07.534
"Read my lips: no new taxes"

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:12.880
It's not uncommon to obsess over the usual&nbsp;suspects for why the US is less equal than Europe

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.319
and also has less good quality affordable social&nbsp;programs.

00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:20.921
The analysis is simple: the US has more billionaires and taxes them less.

00:01:20.921 --> 00:01:23.910
Europe has less&nbsp;billionaires but taxes them more.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:31.205
a larger tax base to fund popular social programs and also less&nbsp;inequality just raise the taxes on the rich like the Europeans do.

00:01:31.205 --> 00:01:37.550
Now although the tax rates for&nbsp;
rich people explain some part of the difference it doesn't explain enough.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:45.045
Europeans can afford good&nbsp;
social programs because a lot of Europeans pay a lot for them, mainly through indirect taxes.

00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:53.120
And&nbsp;Europeans are also more equal before taxes even factored in because a lot of the social programs&nbsp;they pay more for also reduce pre-tax inequality.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.640
Where Americans deal with a sales tax at their&nbsp;final point of sale, Europeans deal with a VAT

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.040
which is a consumption tax that taxes at&nbsp;multiple steps across entire supply chains.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.840
This means that a lot of things in Europe will&nbsp;be more expensive but that public money can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.709
redistributed towards social programs that will&nbsp;reduce before tax inequality even more.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:20.800
But the US has so far been unable to adopt the same. Why? Well, in part because taxing the wealthiest of society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.923
is easier to advertise and justify than taxing&nbsp;everybody a lot more.

00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:29.308
In Europe, most people don't really need to be convinced about paying for their&nbsp;
welfare states

00:02:29.308 --> 00:02:31.643
because it works sufficiently for most of them.

00:02:31.643 --> 00:02:38.801
But in the US, there's an intimate&nbsp;
struggle with deciding whether pre-distribution will be a state local or federal solution.

00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:45.360
Part&nbsp;of the reason is that in the US the federal government gets the most media attention while&nbsp;state and local governments can avoid scrutiny.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.760
This means that minimum wage hikes in the US are&nbsp;practically always seen as a responsibility for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.840
the federal government, which has to delicately&nbsp;balance different costs of living like laborers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.930
living in New York City and minors living in West&nbsp;Virginia.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:03.280
In Europe though, a country like France or Denmark can offer different geographically&nbsp;sensitive policy while also being held more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.520
directly into account because the assignment&nbsp;of responsibility is clear to its citizens.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.320
"There's this definition of a wicked problem which&nbsp;is circulating around in the literature which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:19.840
suggests that a wicked problem is wicked if it has&nbsp;so many dimensions to it that you can't go at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.600
angle to the problem and try to fix it and I think&nbsp;school finances like that so school districts get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:31.120
money from their local communities in the form&nbsp;of property taxes. They'll also get money from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.600
state and the state might give the money through&nbsp;multiple formulas and then a portion a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.320
smaller portion about 9 or 10% comes&nbsp;
from the federal government so even if you try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.000
solve the problem at say, the state level, there are&nbsp;these other funding sources that come with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.285
own restrictions which ultimately affect how the&nbsp;money gets used in schools."

00:03:50.285 --> 00:03:56.000
In Connecticut, a US state, the school in Bridgeport will be poor by&nbsp;virtue of being surrounded by poor properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.665
while school in Greenwich will be richer because&nbsp;it is surrounded by richer properties.

00:03:59.665 --> 00:04:04.217
This is because local taxes are largely funded by property&nbsp;values in the area.

00:04:04.217 --> 00:04:07.123
However this concept fails to explain everything.

00:04:07.123 --> 00:04:12.000
Educational disparities also&nbsp;exist across Europe and within its countries

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.280
The US is not unique for having a system that&nbsp;predisposes poor children to poor resources.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.760
Education systems are complex. Finland is not the&nbsp;same as Sweden. And resources invested in education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.212
and the quality that follows does not seem like&nbsp;a linear relationship.

00:04:27.212 --> 00:04:33.280
The US' unique situation is that its quality does not match the&nbsp;
amount that the US actually spends on it.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.680
One of the problems with answering why resources&nbsp;don't match the quality you would expect in the US

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.520
is because the decentralized nature of&nbsp;
school districts makes it pretty difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.790
to actually identify what spending works and what&nbsp;doesn't.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:51.200
Overall there are some indications that compared with Europe the amount of staffing, the associated health and retirement benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.080
alongside transportation all add up to more&nbsp;costs for the American education system.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.337
But the differences don't end there.

00:04:58.337 --> 00:05:04.771
"In the 1990s,&nbsp;as the crime rate was plummeting, as American life was getting safer and safer,

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:11.274
Americans freaked&nbsp;out and thought that if they take their eyes off their children the children will be abducted.

00:05:11.274 --> 00:05:18.640
The fear was stoked by cable tv in the 1980s, there were a few high-profile&nbsp;
abductions but it's not until the 1990s that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.320
we really start locking kids up and saying&nbsp;"you cannot be outside until you're 14 or 15."&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.000
We took this essential period of childhood from&nbsp;about 8 to 12 when kids throughout history have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.135
practiced independence, have gotten into adventures,&nbsp;have made rafts and floated down the Mississippi&nbsp;River.

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:40.320
We took that period and said: you don't&nbsp;
get to practice independence until it's too late,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.218
until that period is over."

00:05:42.218 --> 00:05:45.440
Kids in the US&nbsp;are raised differently than kids in Europe.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.449
Some blame the planning that produced more&nbsp;isolated American families,

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:52.608
some the over paranoia towards kidnapping in the 1980s,

00:05:52.608 --> 00:05:55.485
and others point&nbsp;to an increasingly competitive environment

00:05:55.485 --> 00:05:59.472
where parents try to give their children an edge over&nbsp;the others by helicoptering them.

00:05:59.472 --> 00:06:06.746
It's really not that far stretch to say that many European&nbsp;children could be considered to have more freedom at an earlier age.

00:06:06.746 --> 00:06:11.661
American children are less&nbsp;likely to be able to walk to school alone compared with European children,

00:06:11.661 --> 00:06:15.460
which you can of course&nbsp;connect to overall crime rates and authoritative school policy

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:19.505
is still much more accepted across&nbsp;
the US than it is in Europe.

00:06:19.505 --> 00:06:22.800
"So the real key to...

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.447
the distinctiveness of America

00:06:27.447 --> 00:06:32.466
is the structure&nbsp;of our government one part of it of course is the independence of the judiciary

00:06:32.466 --> 00:06:39.218
but there's a lot more. There are very few countries in the world for example that have a bicameral&nbsp;legislature.

00:06:39.218 --> 00:06:45.345
England has a House of Lords for the time being but the House of Lords has no&nbsp;substantial power

00:06:45.345 --> 00:06:48.887
they can just make the Commons pass a bill a second time.

00:06:48.887 --> 00:06:53.214
France has a senate,&nbsp;it's honorific. Italy has a senate, it's honorific.

00:06:53.214 --> 00:06:58.720
Very few countries have two separate&nbsp;
bodies in the legislature equally powerful.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.120
That's a lot of trouble as you gentlemen&nbsp;
doubtless know to get the same language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.294
through two different bodies elected in a&nbsp;different fashion.

00:07:09.294 --> 00:07:11.680
Very few countries in the world

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:20.240
have a a separately elected chief executive. Sometimes I go to Europe to talk about separation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:27.040
of powers and when I get there i find that all&nbsp;I'm talking about is independence of the judiciary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:34.320
because the Europeans don't even try to divide the&nbsp;two political powers, the two political branches:

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.371
the legislature and the chief executive.

00:07:37.371 --> 00:07:42.554
In all of&nbsp;the parliamentary countries the chief executive is the creature of the legislature,

00:07:42.554 --> 00:07:49.315
there's never&nbsp;any disagreement between them and the prime minister as there is sometimes between&nbsp;you and the president.

00:07:49.315 --> 00:07:54.967
When there's a disagreement they just kick them out, they have a no-confidence&nbsp;vote a new election

00:07:54.967 --> 00:07:58.575
and they get a prime minister who agrees with the legislature

00:07:58.575 --> 00:08:01.252
And you know&nbsp;the Europeans..."

00:08:01.252 --> 00:08:05.621
The difference is simple: European countries are mainly nations not federations.

00:08:05.621 --> 00:08:10.611
A&nbsp;European state, if it existed, could never be the same as the US.

00:08:10.611 --> 00:08:14.243
Written in their constitution, US&nbsp;
states are modeled after the federal government

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.960
and state governments, which mirror the one above, usually have their own chamber of representatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.488
a senate and also have an executive mirrored&nbsp;after their president

00:08:22.488 --> 00:08:26.022
In Europe, if there was any state that was above the countries that&nbsp;
made it up,

00:08:26.022 --> 00:08:28.678
it would have to be modeled from those very different countries beneath it

00:08:28.678 --> 00:08:31.440
rather&nbsp;than serving as a model for the countries below it.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.760
This is an important difference because it&nbsp;means that any kind of European government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.240
would have to balance parliamentary systems with&nbsp;presidential republics, federal states with unitary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.480
ones and they'll be expected to create a system&nbsp;that hopefully pleases each one sufficiently.&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.600
All in all, although the differences&nbsp;
between the two are practically endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.680
and some are near impossible to actually&nbsp;describe, it's still a worthwhile effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.213
to try and figure a few out and also discuss&nbsp;them.

00:08:57.213 --> 00:09:00.954
In some way, Europe has more breathing room to understand what it wants to be,

00:09:00.954 --> 00:09:04.668
which is no&nbsp;easy task because whatever you think Europe is,

00:09:04.668 --> 00:09:21.840
it can still look across the Atlantic and realize what it definitely is not.

